\title{Instruction Sets and VM Architecture}
\author{dhsorens}
\date{2026-08-18}

\p{
    Whether to prove an existing ISA or design one for provability. RISC-V won the general-purpose end by bringing a compiler toolchain with it; the custom VMs argue the constraint cost is worth the loss.
}

\p{
    Questions this page should answer:
    \ul{
        \li{RISC-V (RV32I and extensions) as a target: what makes each instruction class expensive to constrain.}
        \li{Register machines versus stack machines (Miden, zkWasm), and what the memory argument looks like in each.}
        \li{Purpose-built ISAs — Cairo, Valida — and the specific constraint-cost arguments for them.}
        \li{Instruction decomposition and the [lookup](dhsorens-002W)-based approach to semantics that Jolt takes.}
        \li{The formal ISA specification: what the Sail-to-Lean route gives, and what it does not.}
    }
}
